# update-expected.py --levels L[,L...] (plan 5.17.5, M24): every test is
# built and run at each level, in the order given, and --levels excludes
# --opt. The stand-in compiler Inputs/levels-cc.py does at each level what
# its --at for that level says: it copies a stand-in native program, a .NET
# Framework executable assembled with 'ilasm -exe' that runs directly on
# Windows, as a native program would (Inputs/fake-native-44.il exits with
# 44, Inputs/exit-255.il with 255 and Inputs/fake-native-300.il with 300,
# outside 0..255), or a file that is no program, or it fails as a compile
# does; it also fails at a level it has no --at for, so every run below
# shows which levels it built.
# (1) The same transcript at -O0 and -O2: writing gives 'wrote
#     <stem>.expected' and --check 'ok: <test>'. --check with another
#     transcript at -O2 fails with one 'MISMATCH: <test>' line and the diff
#     of that level alone, whose second file is 'native (windows, -O2)'.
# (2) Writing with different transcripts at -O0 and -O2 fails with
#     'MISMATCH: <test> (the native transcripts at -O0 and -O2 differ;
#     nothing written)' and their diff, and writes nothing.
# (3) A failure at -O2, after -O0 built and ran, names its level: 'FAIL
#     (native compile at -O2)' after the compiler's error, 'FAIL (native run
#     at -O2)' after msil-run.py's, and 'REFUSED: <test>: the native run at
#     -O2 ended with [exit 300: outside 0..255]: ...' (plan D37); each fails
#     with status 1 and writes nothing. With one level the line names none,
#     as without --levels: under --levels O2, and at the -O0 of a run without
#     --levels or --opt (where the stand-in fails, having no --at for -O0).
# (4) Usage errors, with status 2 before anything is built: --opt with
#     --levels, a level that does not exist (in --levels and in --opt), and a
#     level that --levels names twice.
# Windows only: the stand-in native programs are .NET Framework executables.
# REQUIRES: system-windows, msil-triple-windows, msil-netfx

# DEFINE: %{levels_cc} = %python %S/Inputs/levels-cc.py
# RUN: rm -rf %t.d && mkdir -p %t.d
# RUN: %ilasm -exe -output=%t.d/exit-44.exe %S/Inputs/fake-native-44.il %S/Inputs/fake-native-cc.il
# RUN: %ilasm -exe -output=%t.d/exit-255.exe %S/Inputs/exit-255.il
# RUN: %ilasm -exe -output=%t.d/exit-300.exe %S/Inputs/fake-native-300.il %S/Inputs/fake-native-cc.il
# RUN: %{harness_check} write %t.d/no-program.exe "levels-cc: this file is no program"
# RUN: %{harness_check} write %t.d/prog.c "// The stand-in compiler ignores this source." "// RUN: %%{msil_c}" "int main(void) { return 0; }"

